{"acceptance_length": 4.163265306122449, "acceptance_lengths": [2, 6, 3, 5, 7, 3, 2, 1, 6, 5, 6, 1, 6, 8, 1, 8, 8, 3, 6, 5, 2, 1, 3, 1, 4, 1, 1, 2, 5, 8, 1, 8, 8, 8, 8, 8, 8, 8, 8, 8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2], "accepted_draft_lengths": [1, 5, 2, 4, 6, 2, 1, 0, 5, 4, 5, 0, 5, 7, 0, 7, 7, 2, 5, 4, 1, 0, 2, 0, 3, 0, 0, 1, 4, 7, 0, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 22851418112, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 22851408384, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "You can write a function to check if a tuple contains **no duplicates** by comparing the length of the tuple with the length of a set made from the tuple. A set automatically removes duplicates, so if the lengths are different, there are duplicates.\n\nHere's the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef check_distinct(t):\n    return len(t) == len(set(t))\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- `set(t)` creates a set from the tuple, which removes any duplicate values.\n- If the length of the original tuple and the set are the same, it means all elements are unique.\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert check_distinct((1, 4, 5, 6, 1, 4)) == False\nassert check_distinct((1, 4, 5, 6)) == True\nassert check_distinct((2, 3, 4, 5, 6)) == True\n```\n\nAll of these assertions will pass with this implementation.", "decode_seconds": 2.7213033149018884, "draft_calls": 49, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 129, "method": "native_target_eagle3", "output_hash": "6704d2ebb3637f2a1c71a2532af0e4eec0ebe88487719dfb714e15f49022fdf1", "output_tokens": 205, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 22978688512, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 23358078976, "problem_id": "Mbpp/394", "profile_regions_ms": {"draft": 1117.781504034996, "prefill_full_target": 34.1616325378418, "prefill_relay": 0.4016000032424927, "relay": 15.559904009103775, "unattributed_runtime": 39.52571327239275, "verification_full_target": 1513.8729610443115}, "proposal_lengths": [7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7